# PUBLIC Quarto and PRIVATE Knowledge Graph


## Step 1: Obsidian Setup


## Step 2: Quarto Setup

Run the creation command directly inside your root workspace directory. Use the --no-run-runtime flag to prevent it from stalling on environment checks, and specify the website type:

```bash
quarto create project website .
```
What to expect: Quarto will scan the directory, see your existing setup, and generate its boilerplate configuration files. It will explicitly create or attempt to overwrite _quarto.yml, index.qmd, about.qmd, styles.css, and .gitignore.

### Step 2 Extra: Use Git to Monitor Overwritten Configurations

Because Quarto's default templates will update the specific path exclusions needed to shield your private brain/ graph and .obsidian configurations, use Git immediately to inspect.

```bash
PS Q:\__writing__\_LLM_wiki_\LLM-wiki-quarto> quarto create project website .
? Title (website) » LLM-wiki-quarto
Creating project at Q:\__writing__\_LLM_wiki_\LLM-wiki-quarto:
  - Created _quarto.yml
  - Created .gitignore
  - Created index.qmd
  - Created about.qmd
  - Created styles.css
```

## Step 3: Quarto Publication via Github Workflows

(... the green **`✓ DNS check successful`** or Github Page ...)

### 1. Enable Workflow Permissions on GitHub

Grant your repository's automated runner permission to write the compiled web assets back to your repository:

1. Click the repo **Settings** gear tab.
2. Look at the left sidebar, scroll down to **Actions**, and click **General**.
3. Scroll down to the bottom section named **Workflow permissions**.
4. Select **"Read and write permissions"**, then click **Save**.

### 2. Create the GitHub Action Workflow File

Create a nested system folder path: `.github/workflows/`.

Run these quick setup commands in your PowerShell terminal:

```powershell
# Create the standard hidden GitHub automated actions folder structure
mkdir -p .github/workflows

# Create the deployment workflow file configuration
New-Item -Path ".github\workflows" -Name "publish.yml" -ItemType "file"

```

Open that brand-new **`.github/workflows/publish.yml`** file in your code editor and paste this configuration block:

```yaml
name: Quarto Publish and Deploy

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ] # Triggers the build automatically whenever you push to main

jobs:
  build-deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
      pages: write
      id-token: write # <--- CRITICAL: Permission to verify the runner
      
    steps:
      - name: Check out repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Quarto 
        uses: quarto-dev/quarto-actions/setup@v2

      - name: Render Quarto Project
        uses: quarto-dev/quarto-actions/render@v2

      - name: Upload Artifact
        uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
        with:
          path: _site  # This is where Quarto puts the HTML

      - name: Deploy to GitHub Pages
        id: deployment
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4   

```

---

### 3. Commit and Fire It Off

Commit it, and push it up to execute the automation:

```powershell
git add .
git commit -m "Add automated GitHub Actions publishing pipeline"
git push origin main

```
